WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.040
Hey, Wisecrack.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.220
Jared incarnate again,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.760
and we’ve got another Wisecrack quick take on this week’s Rick and Morty episode, "Pickle Rick."

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.460
"I'm Pickle Rick!"

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.660
I’m here to try to answer the question:
why does Rick turn into a pickle?

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:20.140
And yes, it’s partly because Dan Harmon said, “Rick is a pickle” in a writer’s room brainstorm.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.580
"And Harmon just goes, 'Oh, and uh Rick is a pickle!'"

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.590
And then there’s the other easy answer to
avoid family therapy.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:28.990
But if you stick with me, I’m going
to argue that Rick’s being-as-pickle can

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.260
teach us a lot about freedom.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.000
Welcome to this Wisecrack Quick Take on Pickle Rick.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.970
And, of course, spoilers ahead.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:40.360
But first, a recap: Rick, if you couldn’t
deduce, has turned himself into a pickle to

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.830
get out of family therapy.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:46.150
But things go awry, and he ends up fighting
his way out of a sewer, and later, a Russian

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:49.770
embassy using a mech suit made out of roach
parts and rat limbs.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.360
Meanwhile, Beth, Summer and Morty have to
go to family therapy after Summer huffed pottery

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.190
enamel and Morty peed his pants.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.720
There’s a clever parallel between Beth and
Rick in this episode.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.829
Just as Rick goes to insane lengths to avoid
baring his emotions in therapy, Beth is doing

00:01:04.829 --> 00:01:10.200
her best to stonewall her school-appointed
therapist and maintain her emotional distance.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:14.420
“I think it's possible that you and your father have a very, uh, specific dynamic.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.620
I don't think it's one that rewards emotion or vulnerability. I think it may punish them.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.300
I think it's possible that dynamic eroded your marriage

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.060
and is infecting your kids with the tendency to misdirect their feelings."

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.240
"F**k you."

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:27.740
"Mom!"

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.060
"F**k both of you, too."

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:33.460
One thing we’ve noticed in this season is that there’s more emphasis on serialized arcs.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.660
In the last episode, Rick is willing to stay
in Mad Max land to avoid his daughter’s divorce.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.900
"My daughter's going through a divorce, and I am not dealing with it in a healthy way at all."

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:48.020
Now, he’s literally turned himself into
a pickle to run away from reality.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.990
And Morty and Summer seem increasingly damaged
by their parents divorce.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:59.080
"I am mad that I can't huff enamel without people assuming it's because my family sucks."

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.800
With this continuation, we’ve noticed a
few themes that seem to be slowly developing.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.509
One of which is freedom.

00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:08.780
This quote from Dr. Wong really sets the stage
for our discussion:

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:15.359
"I think it's because the only truly unapproachable concept for you is that it's your mind within your control.

00:02:15.359 --> 00:02:17.069
You chose to come here.

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:18.109
You chose to talk.

00:02:18.109 --> 00:02:22.079
To belittle my vocation, just as you chose
to become a pickle.

00:02:22.079 --> 00:02:24.069
Each of us gets to choose."

00:02:24.069 --> 00:02:27.489
At first, Dr. Wong’s monologue seemed a
little strange.

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:30.459
After all, how could Rick not understand that
he’s in control?

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:33.420
He can do anything, including turn himself
into a pickle.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.080
"The reason anyone would do this, if they could —&nbsp;which they can't —

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.220
would be because they could —&nbsp;which they can't."

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.519
But after digging through my notes on the
ugliest of existential philosophers, I think

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:46.739
Dr. Wong’s monologue gets us to something
quite insightful.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.180
As we all know, Rick doesn’t believe in
God.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.600
"There is no God, Summer. Gotta rip that band-aid off now, you'll thank me later."

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:57.640
And for philosopher Jean Paul Sartre, godlessness
means we are radically free to define our

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.640
own existence.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.749
But, instead, we choose to do things based
on social pressures, or who we think we’re

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:04.700
supposed to be, and all kinds of other bullsh*t.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.620
Sartre calls this acting in "bad faith."

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.800
But since we’re mortal, there's a fixed
amount of choices to make in our lives, what

00:03:11.809 --> 00:03:13.840
Sartre calls "finitude."

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.099
And each of those choices prevents other choices:
choosing to be truck driver means choosing

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:19.459
not to be a teacher.

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:24.059
To deny the the importance of our finite choices
is another way to act in bad faith.

00:03:24.059 --> 00:03:26.640
Often we pretend to not have a choice.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.180
As West Side Story puts it:

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:34.720
“Dear kindly Sergeant Krupke, you gotta understand, it's just our bringin' up-ke that gets us out of hand.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:40.980
Our mothers all are junkies, our fathers all are drunks! Golly Moses, naturally we're punks!"

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.299
Essentially, the hoodlums know that they’re
acting like a bunch of little shits, but instead

00:03:44.299 --> 00:03:48.249
of choosing to be better, they relinquish
their choice and blame it on their negligent parents.

00:03:48.249 --> 00:03:50.010
"That's why I'm so bad!"

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.510
They just can’t help themselves.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.220
But what’s unique about Rick is that he
doesn’t deal with finitude.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.340
He deals with infinitude.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.559
"My access to infinite timelines precludes a necessity of attachment."

00:04:00.559 --> 00:04:04.609
Rick has to grapple with the exact opposite
of what Sartre proposed, because, to call

00:04:04.609 --> 00:04:05.600
back to season 1:

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.030
"There's an infinite number of realities, Morty!"

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:12.370
Whereas Sartre criticizes the person who won’t
take responsibility for their choices, Rick

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.329
thinks he doesn’t have to, because he has
already made them all in the infinite versions

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:17.389
of himself.

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:22.190
If Sartre said the human condition was one
of finite choices, well, Rick has transcended

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.300
that human condition with science.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:29.160
But as Dr. Wong suggests, justifying your
actions with the multiverse, or your god-like

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.390
intelligence, isn’t a solution.

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:36.140
Because although intelligence can help you
make a decision, you still have to make the decision.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:41.100
"The only connection between your unquestionable intelligence and the sickness destroying your family,

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.960
is that everyone in your family, &nbsp;you included,&nbsp;use intelligence to justify sickness."

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:50.560
Despite the fact that Rick is the “master
of his universe,” he paradoxically has yet

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.570
to come to terms with his own choices.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:57.000
No matter how many versions of Rick exist,
nothing can stop the fact that his choices

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.900
are uniquely his own.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.440
Because to quote Dr. Wong,

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:05.580
"The only truly unapproachable concept for you is that it's your mind within your control."

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.260
In season 1, Rick straight up tells us how
he deals with infinite choice in the multiverse.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.440
"What about the reality where Hitler cured cancer, Morty? The answer is 'don't think about it!'"

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:19.260
But of course, all therapy does is make you
think about things, so Rick turns himself into

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.120
a pickle to avoid choice, or at least the
appearance of it.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.030
As a helpless, armless pickle, it’s not
his fault if he can’t make therapy..

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:32.170
And in that sense, Rick’s blatant disregard
for his choices, as Sartre would say, is acting

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:33.400
in bad faith.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.250
Speaking of arcs, this ties in nicely to what
we talked about in the last episode..

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:41.570
Between Jerry’s powerlessness, Morty’s
resentment of him for that very reason, and

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.560
the juxtaposition with Rick as an all powerful
madman —

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.000
"I don't respect therapy because I'm a scientist.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.300
Because I invent, transform, create, and destroy for a living, and when I don't like something about the world,

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:51.978
I change it!"

00:05:51.978 --> 00:05:53.700
This season is really leaning into

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.130
ideas of agency, and I’m super excited to
see what they come up with next.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:01.050
There’s one last thing worth noting, but
it’s probably more of a stretch.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:04.870
You know the common characterization of Freud’s
work as “everything is a penis?”

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:09.700
Well, I’m convinced someone in their writer’s
room probably took this loose connection between a phallic

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:12.700
object and therapy and went a little wild with it.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:15.920
But if you want to hear more about this, you’ll
want to check it out the Wisecrack podcast,

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:19.200
where we're breaking down every new episode of Rick
and Morty.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:23.200
Ryan, Alec, and I talk further about all the
intricacies of this awesome episode.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.020
So, check us out on iTunes, or wherever you
get your podcasts.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.900
Thanks a lot for all the support, guys. Thanks for all the recommendations.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.940
Thanks for all the feedback. We really appreciate it.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:32.460
See ya later.

